    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_815c70f877fbbab2249e73a0cfecec92a7dabf3d44d1e9d4402ec397465e76a4",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The source refines the circle-method partition by shrinking the rational neighborhoods used as major arcs, then assigns the integral over those narrowed arcs to the leading term and the complement to the remainder. This relocates the analytic boundary: precise Hardy-Littlewood analysis is reserved for the tightly structured region, while a uniform GRH-dependent estimate for S(a), obtained through summation over zeros of L-functions, controls the enlarged remainder. The remainder lemma is stated for every N greater than 10^20, explicitly without requiring N to be odd. The refinement gains a cleaner division between approximation and bounding, but its success still depends on GRH and on the unproved-in-this-span leading-term and remainder estimates; damaged endpoints and the numerical S(a) bound are withheld.",
      "parent_sources": [
